# northstar — Architecture North Star, Language Grammar, AST & Type System Specification
File: openOODA/northstar.oot

This document defines the target architecture, formal grammar, AST design, and type system.
Version SSoT is git tags plus per-repo VERSION files; see rules.oot §1.21 and rfcs/0006.
For the current per-repo tag list, run `git ls-remote --tags https://github.com/openOODA/<repo>`
or `bin/ooda version`.

openOODA is a systems programming language engineered for safe, high-performance, deterministic software.
The compiler verifies source code ahead-of-time.
All side effects and privileged operations require explicit capabilities.
Functions declare verifiable contracts (`requires`, `ensures`, `spec`).
The compiler produces machine-readable diagnostics with automated one-turn repairs.

## Target — Verifiable Computation Everywhere

No trust without proof, on any hardware.
Capabilities replace trusted programs. Code touches only what it is handed.
Contracts replace trusted behavior. Functions state what they guarantee.
Reproducible builds replace trusted binaries. The artifact is what the source says.
A 256-byte Stage0 seed and a Stage A / Stage B digest match are that proof (section 5.3).
Double-run tests and flight records replace trusted test results. Even evidence is verified.
Self-hosting replaces trusted vendors. The toolchain builds itself.
Multi-target (CPU, AMD/NVIDIA/Intel GPUs, WASM) replaces trusted hardware.
Per-target 10/10 scores measure how completely trust has been replaced by evidence.

## 1. Architectural Foundation

### 1.1 Architectural Layer Model (9-Org Ecosystem: Root `@openOODA` + L0-L7 & Toolchain Strata L0.1-L0.4)
Each layer stabilizes a deterministic foundation for the layer above it across the 9 GitHub organizations (`@openOODA` umbrella + L0-L7):
- **Root Umbrella (`@openOODA`, 6 repos — Governance, Buildbox, Website, Status, Installer, Package Catalog & Org CI)**: `openOODA` (24 laws, RFCs, `buildbox/`), `website` (`openooda.org`), `status` (`status.openooda.org`), `install`, `catalog`, and `.github`.
- **Layer 0 (`@openOODA-core`, 10 repos -> 9 canonical repos — Toolchain, Compiler & Runtime Substrate)**:
  - **L0.1 (Host/Silicon ABI Substrate)**: Syscall & hardware bridge (`openOODA-core/oodar/`; `oo_*` symbols across 6 per-domain subdirs per the v2.0.0 layout).
  - **L0.2 (Deterministic Runtime & Memory)**: Linear arena allocators with O(1) bulk resets, deterministic ARC, and Landlock isolation (`openOODA-core/oodar/`, `openOODA-core/oostd/`, and `openOODA-core/oostd-hw/`).
  - **L0.3 (Self-Hosted Compiler & IR)**: Lexer, parser, Merkle AST, SSA mem2reg, optimization passes, and direct ELF64/PE32+/WASM emission (`openOODA-core/oodac/`).
  - **L0.4 (Language Semantics, Stdlib & Developer Tools)**: ADTs, 32 explicit capabilities (64-bit cycle register), refinement types, typestates, SECRET taint tracking, SMT contracts, `oostd`, `oostd-hw`, `oocli` (absorbs `ooda`), `oopm`, `oolsp`, `oomcp`, and `ootui`.
- **Layer 1 (`@openOODA-kernel`, 16 repos -> 6 canonical repos — Bare-Metal Microkernel, HAL & Drivers)**: UEFI `ooboot` (from `ookernel-boot`), `x86_64`/`aarch64`/`riscv64` `oohal` (from `ookernel-hal-x86`, absorbs `arm64`/`riscv`), NT `Ob` + seL4 `CSpace` hybrid microkernel `ookernel` (from `ookernel-sched`, absorbs `mm`/`ipc`/`ob`/`iommu`/`unikernel`), and Ring-3 drivers `oodrv-storage` (from `oodrv-nvme`), `oodrv-io` (from `oodrv-net`, absorbs `usb-hid`/`display-gop`/`pci`), and `oodrv-virt` (from `oodrv-virtio`).
- **Layer 2 (`@openOODA-OS`, 18 repos -> 9 canonical daemons — Zero-Root System Daemons & Container Builder)**: `ooinit` (absorbs `oosvc-mgr`), `oovfs` (absorbs `oofs-ext4`/`oofs-tmp`), `oonetd` (absorbs `oodnsd`), `oosysbus` (absorbs `oopowerd`/`oologd`/`ooaudiod`), `ooauthd` (from `oosession`, absorbs `oopolicyd`/`oosandbox`), `oohealthd`, `oocontainer` (from `ooimage`, absorbs `oorealm`/`ooupdate`), `oollama` (absorbs `ooaid`), and `oodevd`.
- **Layer 3 (`@openOODA-tools`, 257 repos -> 64 canonical multi-call utilities — Userland CLI / TUI / MCP Utilities)**: Capability-sandboxed userland utilities across 12 functional categories (`oosh`, `oogrep`, `oofind`, `oojq`, `oocurl`, `oodiff`, `oohash` [absorbs `oob3sum`/`oosha256`/`oosha512`/`oomd5`/`oocksum`], `oocodec` [from `oobase64`], `ooarchive` [absorbs `ootar`/`oogzip`/`ooxz`/`oozstd`/`oounzip`], `ootheme` [from `oote`], `ootext`, `ooquery` [absorbs `oosqlite`], etc.) using clean CLI subcommand dispatch, with no `--persona` / `--mode` / `--list-personas` flags, symlink, or hardlink alias for any persona (`rfcs/0012-unix-less-is-more-consolidation.oot`, `persona-ownership.oot`, `rules.oot` section 1.25).
- **Layer 4 (`@openOODA-DE`, 20 repos -> 8 canonical desktop components — Capability Wayland Desktop Environment)**: `oocomp` (absorbs `oowm`/`oowall`/`oolock`), `oopanel` (absorbs `oolaunch`/`ooshelf`/`ootoast`/`ootaskmgr`/`oocockpit`), `ooportal` (absorbs `ooclip`/`ooshot`), `ooterm`, `oofiles` (absorbs `ooview`/`ooedit`), `oosettings`, `oobazaar`, and `ooagent-hud`.
- **Layer 5 (`@openOODA-apps`, 18 repos -> 10 canonical applications — Flagship GUI Applications & Creator Suite)**: `oobrowser`, `oostudio` (absorbs `oogit-ui`), `oooffice` (from `oooffice`, absorbs `oosheet`/`oodeck`/`oonotes`/`oopdf`), `oocanvas` (absorbs `oopaint`), `oomedia`, `ooflash` (standalone bootable USB/ISO image flasher), `oodaw`, `oochat` (absorbs `oomail`), `oopass`, and `ookvm` (from `ookvm`).
- **Layer 6 (`@openOODA-cloud`, 18 repos -> 8 canonical distributed engines — Distributed Cluster, Mesh & GPU Compute Grid)**: `oomesh` (absorbs `oodns-cloud`/`oorelay`), `oostore` (absorbs `oostore-cas`/`ooregistry-cloud`), `oodeploy` (absorbs `oobuild-fleet`/`ooinfer-grid`/`oosched-cloud`), `oogateway` (absorbs `oocdn`), `ooidentity` (absorbs `ooconsent`), `oodb` (from `oodb-dist`, absorbs `ooqueue`), `ootelemetry`, and `oobilling`.
- **Layer 7 (`@openOODA-agents`, 16 repos -> 9 canonical OODA engines — Autonomous OODA Loops, Swarm, OVS Benchmark & Governance)**: `ooloop` (from `oooda-engine`, absorbs `ooplanner`), `ooswarm` (absorbs `oorouter` and `oomux`), `oobench` (from `ooeval`, canonical OODA Value Score [`OVS`] benchmark engine), `ooquota`, `oofalsify` (absorbs `ooredteam`), `ooconsensus` (absorbs `ooevolve`), `oocode` (absorbs `ooreviewer`), `oomemory`, and `oogovern` (absorbs `ooarchitect`/`oorelease`).

### 1.2 Memory & Concurrency Model
- Linear arena allocators provide zero-heap standard library operations with bulk resets.
- Automatic Reference Counting (ARC) reclaims memory deterministically when reference counts reach zero.
- Buffer slicing operates with zero memory copies.
- Actor-based concurrency isolates memory heaps behind typed, bounded channels.
- Speculative execution uses transactional rollback buffers before commits.

## 2. Target Language Vision (Non-Normative Architecture Grammar)

> **CRITICAL GOVERNANCE NOTICE FOR AI CODING AGENTS & IMPLEMENTERS**:
> This section is an architectural NORTH STAR and is STRICTLY NON-NORMATIVE.
> It outlines future research directions and conceptual grammar (traits, newtypes, where-predicates).
> Do NOT use this section for compiler implementation, code generation, or syntax validation.
> The SOLE AUTHORITATIVE NORMATIVE LANGUAGE SPECIFICATIONS are:
> 1. Tier A Language Spec Sheet: `openOODA/spec.oot` (concise invariant rules)
> 2. Tier B Normative Codex: `openOODA/docs/spec/` (modules 01_lexical.oot through 08_runtime_abi.oot)
> In active product code (`.oo`):
> - NO traits, newtypes, or where-clauses exist in the active compiler.
> - Imports MUST be relative string literals (e.g. `import "file.oo";`), NEVER `::` namespaces.
> - NO struct trailing commas are permitted (`RULES §1.11`).
> - NO parentheses around condition blocks (`if cond { ... }`, `while cond { ... }`).

### 2.1 Core Syntax Rules (Aspirational Vision)
- Bindings are immutable by default (`let x = val;`); mutable bindings require `let mut x = val;`.
- Expressions yield values: blocks, `if/else`, and `match` expressions return results.
- The language contains no null pointers; use `Option[T]` and `Result[T, E]`.
- Handlers must explicitly process `Result` and `Option` returns using `match` or `?`.
- Functions declare contract clauses (`requires`, `ensures`, `spec`).

### 2.2 Formal EBNF Grammar (Non-Normative Target Model)
```
Program           ::= { Item } ;
Item              ::= FunctionDecl | TypeDecl | TraitDecl | ImportDecl ;
ImportDecl        ::= "import" Identifier [ "::" Identifier ] ";" ;
TypeDecl          ::= "type" Identifier "=" ( SumType | StructType | RefinementType | Newtype ) ";" ;
SumType           ::= Variant { "|" Variant } ;
Variant           ::= Identifier [ "(" Type ")" ] ;
StructType        ::= "struct" "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl         ::= Identifier ":" Type [ "," ] ;
RefinementType    ::= Type "where" Expression ;
Newtype           ::= "newtype" Type ;
FunctionDecl      ::= [ "pub" ] "fn" Identifier "(" [ ParameterList ] ")" [ "->" Type ]
                      [ ContractSpec ] Block [ VerifyBlock ] ;
ParameterList     ::= Parameter { "," Parameter } ;
Parameter         ::= Identifier ":" [ "&" ] Type ;
ContractSpec      ::= { Precondition | Postcondition | SpecClause } ;
Precondition      ::= "requires" Expression ;
Postcondition     ::= "ensures" Expression ;
SpecClause        ::= "spec" StringLiteral ;
VerifyBlock       ::= "verify" Identifier "{" { Statement } "}" ;
Statement         ::= LetStmt | ReturnStmt | ExprStmt | WhileStmt | AssignStmt ;
LetStmt           ::= "let" [ "mut" ] Identifier [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression ";" ;
ReturnStmt        ::= "return" [ Expression ] ";" ;
ExprStmt          ::= Expression ";" ;
WhileStmt         ::= "while" Expression Block ;
AssignStmt        ::= Identifier "=" Expression ";" ;
Expression        ::= IfExpr | MatchExpr | BinaryExpr | CallExpr | PrimaryExpr ;
IfExpr            ::= "if" Expression Block [ "else" ( Block | IfExpr ) ] ;
MatchExpr         ::= "match" Expression "{" { MatchArm } "}" ;
MatchArm          ::= Pattern "=>" ( Expression "," | Block ) ;
Pattern           ::= Identifier [ "(" Identifier ")" ] | Literal | "_" ;
CallExpr          ::= PrimaryExpr "(" [ ArgList ] ")" [ "?" ] ;
```

## 3. Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) Design

### 3.1 Tree Hierarchy & Strongly Typed Nodes
- Root `Program` node contains an ordered sequence of `Item` nodes.
- Strongly typed nodes represent statements, expressions, patterns, types, and contracts.
- Every AST node preserves byte-range source coordinates (file, line, column, length) for diagnostics.
- Function and call nodes maintain resolved capability signatures.

### 3.2 Pure Compile-Time Macro Expansion
- Compile-time macro functions evaluate during compilation.
- Macro functions ingest AST subtrees and emit rewritten AST nodes before code generation.
- Macro expansions cannot introduce out-of-scope identifiers or bypass capability limits.

### 3.3 Merkle AST Digests & Incremental Caching
- Compute cryptographic Merkle digests over normalized syntactic subtrees.
- Incremental compilation skips re-parsing unchanged subtrees via the Merkle cache.
- Linear zero-copy memory layout enables fast disk persistence and queries (`ooda outline`).

## 4. Type System Specifications

### 4.1 Algebraic Data Types & Exhaustive Matching
- Sum Types: Tagged unions with payload parameters (`type Shape = Circle(Float) | Rect(Float, Float);`).
- Product Types: Fixed-layout record structures (`type User = struct { id: u64, name: String };`).
- Core ADTs: Standard library primitives `Option[T]` and `Result[T, E]`.
- Pattern Matching: `match` expressions must exhaustively handle all variants.

### 4.2 Static Capability Verification (32 Explicit Capabilities, 64-Bit Cycle Register)
- 32 explicit capabilities (19 Software/OS + 13 Hardware): `FsReadCap`, `FsWriteCap`, `NetCap`, `TcpCap`,
  `UdpCap`, `SysCap`, `ProcessCap`, `EnvCap`, `TimeCap`, `RandCap`, `AllocCap`, `ThreadCap`, `GpuCap`,
  `FfiCap`, `FsCap`, `ArenaCap`, `MetricsCap`, `SignCap`, `BindCap`, `CompilerReadCap`, `AudioCap`,
  `CameraCap`, `UsbCap`, `HidCap`, `WindowCap`, `FrameCap`, `MmioCap`, `DmaCap`, `IrqCap`, `PciCap`,
  `BlockCap`, `TpmCap`. Bit positions `0..31` are mapped in `oodar/sec/cap/cap_table.json` and `caps.h`.
- 64-bit capability register (`32 + 32 = 64`): lower 32 bits encode the 32 root capabilities (`WHAT`, matching a 32-lane GPU warp); upper 32 bits encode 16-bit OODA Epoch + 8-bit Rights Mask + 8-bit Scope Badge (`HOW & WHEN`).
- Functions accept borrowed capability references (`&FsReadCap`) without taking ownership.
- Capability attenuation allows capability holders to derive strictly narrowed subsets.
- `oo_cap_privacy_attenuate(parent_reg64, child_badge)` in `oostd/sec/capreg.oo`
  packs a child register. The epoch stays. badge8 becomes `child_badge`
  masked to 8 bits. Rights are the parent rights with bits 64 and 128 set.
  Bit 64 is RIGHT_PRIVACY_REDACT. Bit 128 is RIGHT_ZERO_TELEMETRY.
  The substrate macros at those values are SEAL and IMMUTABLE.
  The child mask clears NetCap (0), MetricsCap (11), TcpCap (17),
  UdpCap (18), AudioCap (20), CameraCap (21), HidCap (23), and
  FrameCap (25). TlsCap shares bit 17 with TcpCap.
- Calling privileged functions without valid capabilities fails compilation (`E_CAP`).

### 4.3 Typestate Protocol Verification
- Types parameterize abstract lifecycle states (`File[Unopened]`, `File[Opened]`, `File[Closed]`).
- Methods consume the input typestate and return the successor typestate to prevent invalid sequences.

### 4.4 Refinement Types & Newtypes (Non-Normative Target Vision)
- Refinement Types: Base types qualified by logical predicate expressions (`type Port = Int where val >= 1 && val <= 65535;`). Aspirational future direction.
- Newtypes: Zero-cost type abstractions that provide distinct identities to primitive values. Aspirational future direction.

### 4.5 Static Information Flow Control (IFC) & SECRET Taint
- `SECRET:` annotations tag sensitive data fields at the type level.
- Taint analysis tracks dataflow through assignments, struct packing, and function calls.
- Compiler prevents tainted values from reaching unsealed sinks (`process_exec`, unencrypted I/O).

### 4.6 SMT Decidability Limits
- Restrict verification contracts to decidable SMT logics (QF_LIA for linear arithmetic,
  QF_BV for bitvector operations) with deterministic solver timeouts.

## 5. Compiler Backends & Execution Targets

### 5.1 Backend Hierarchy
- Direct ELF64 Emission: Pure `.oo` backend emitting standalone ELF64 and WASM binaries directly.
- C Code Emission: Generates portable C code linking the oodar runtime (sourced from
  `openOODA-core/oodar/`, v2.0.0 layout; canonical single-TU build is `gcc -c oodar.c`).
- LLVM IR Generation: Direct LLVM IR generation for optimization passes and SIMD vectorization.
- WebAssembly Target: Compiles to sandboxed WASM for browser and edge environments.
- Bare-Metal Target: Minimal runtime profile for embedded architectures (x86_64, RISC-V).

### 5.2 Build Determinism & Data Layouts
- Bit-identical compilation produces reproducible binaries with normalized paths and sorted symbols.
- Support for Struct-of-Arrays (SoA) layout enables high-throughput vector processing.

### 5.3 Stage0 seed and Diverse Double-Compilation

`oostd/sec/ddc_seed.oo` is the Stage0 seed and Diverse Double-Compilation
gate. `oodac/check/ddc_seed.oo` forwards the same four checks.
`oo_ddc_req_caps` requires CompilerReadCap, SignCap, and TpmCap.

`oo_hex0_seed_verify(seed_bytes, seed_digest, elf_init_array_cnt)`
returns -13 when `seed_bytes` is not 256, `seed_digest` is not
positive, or `elf_init_array_cnt` is not 0. A pass returns
`seed_digest`. The bound is one 256-byte hex0 page. A non-zero
`.init_array` count is a pre-main hook and returns -13.

`oo_ddc_compare(stage_a_digest, stage_b_digest, std_merkle)` returns
-13 when either digest is not positive, the oostd/sec Merkle root is
not positive, or the two digests differ. Stage A is the self-hosted
binary. Stage B is the hex0-seeded binary. A match returns
`oo_pqc_sha3_round(stage_a_digest, std_merkle)`.

`oo_ddc_seal_gated` calls `oo_cap_reg64_check` for that mask. A failed
check returns status -13, binary digest 0, and seal 0, and it keeps
the caller seed length and Merkle root. The seal calls
`oo_hex0_seed_verify` with `elf_init_array_cnt` 0. A failed seed
check or a failed compare returns that status, binary digest 0, and
seal 0. A pass sets status 0, `seed_bytes` 256, and `binary_digest`
to the Stage A digest. `ddc` is the compare result. The seal is
`oo_pqc_sha3_round(oo_pqc_sha3_round(seed_digest, ddc), reg64 ^ epoch)`.

`oodac_hex0_seed_verify`, `oodac_ddc_compare`, and `oodac_ddc_seal_gated`
return the oostd results. The init-array count is visible on
`oodac_hex0_seed_verify`. The review record is `qa/review-queue.oot`.

